  you> [machine] goodbye
  exit code: 0

Journal written by that session (6 records, whole file):

  seq 0  session.start   (source.note "main.py live", machine.pid 2898)
  seq 1  counts
  seq 2  nodes.added
  seq 3  edges.added
  seq 4  counts
  seq 5  session.stop    reason "process exited"

Viewer state after the Machine process exited (GET /api/status):

  nodes 28, edges 12, counts.rule_total 12, applied 6, last_seq 5,
  stopped "process exited", machine pid 2898, resets 1

A second, longer run in the same setup (load pack arithmetic, show goal,
suggest lemmas) produced 23 journal records and was seen live by the viewer:

  while the process was running: 16 events seen, 28 items, 12 edges, 12 rules

So the viewer holds genuine events while the REPL is open, the graph changes
while the user types, and the recording closes with the process.

## 3. Without --observe nothing is written

  $ python cat_theo_machine/main.py live --live-snapshot /tmp/fresh3.json   (quit)
  exit 0 in 14.0 s
  stdout: ['[machine] Starting a fresh live graph; ...', 'you> [machine] goodbye']
  journal exists: False

Live mode behaves exactly as before; the flag is the only difference.

## 4. The observed process stays in charge

The tap runs as a daemon thread that only reads graph.nodes, graph.edges and
the rule order through public accessors. No HTTP request is made from the
Machine, no port is opened (live mode still has no inspector port; --port is
inspect-only, default 8765), and the viewer keeps working after the Machine
exits because it only follows the journal file.

## 5. Known limitation, measured: a graph full of live-proof bundles

The default live snapshot here is 6.2 MB and carries `live-proof-*` and
`observed-structure-*` bundle nodes. The digest gives every node a stable id
taken from PrettyTerm, and those bundle terms are expensive to render.

  restored graph: 10 nodes, 0 edges, 132 rules in the graph rule order
  PrettyTerm per node: 18.9s, >20s, 0.8s, >20s, 6.3s, 0.8s, 0.7s, 7.1s, 0.9s, 0.7s
  GraphDigest(...).read(): did not finish within 300 s

  same snapshot, no --observe : boots and quits in 30.2 s (exit 0)
  same snapshot, --observe    : journal still holds only session.start after
                                240 s; the `quit` sent at t=120 s was not
                                honoured within the next 120 s; killed by hand

The cause of the slow poll is the text rendering above; the shutdown behaviour
was not isolated further. Ordinary sessions (packs, facts, rules, goals) poll in
milliseconds — the run in section 2 was interactive throughout. Documented in
visualiser/README.md under Known limitations. No label budget was added: ids
are derived from the rendered text, so a partial render would either move ids
or emit false items.removed records.

